---
import BaseLayout from "@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const faqs = [
  {
    question: "What is the VisaScreen?",
    answer: "It is the health-care worker certificate that U.S. federal law requires from nurses and other professionals educated outside the United States before they can receive a work visa, temporary or permanent. It is issued by TruMerit (formerly CGFNS), the USCIS-authorized credentialing organization, and it verifies your education, your licenses, your English proficiency and — for registered nurses — that you passed the NCLEX-RN."
  },
  {
    question: "How much does the VisaScreen cost?",
    answer: "The initial application costs $740 USD according to TruMerit's official fee schedule, and it already includes the digital certificate. A paper certificate is a separate service with an additional cost. Always confirm the current amount on TruMerit's fee schedule before paying."
  },
  {
    question: "Which English tests does the VisaScreen accept?",
    answer: "IELTS Academic, TOEFL iBT, Pearson PTE Academic, OET, Cambridge (B2 First, C1 Advanced or C2 Proficiency) and MET, with the minimum scores HRSA published on May 12, 2026. At-home versions of any test are not accepted. You are exempt if your nursing education was completed in English in the U.S., the United Kingdom, Australia, Canada (except Quebec), New Zealand or Ireland."
  },
  {
    question: "How long does the VisaScreen take?",
    answer: "TruMerit states it issues certificates within up to 3.5 business days once it has received and validated all documents from the source. Your total processing time depends on how fast your school sends transcripts and your licensing authorities sign the validations — steps TruMerit does not control."
  },
  {
    question: "Do I need the VisaScreen before taking the NCLEX?",
    answer: "No. It is the other way around: if you are a registered nurse (RN), the VisaScreen requires that you have already passed the NCLEX-RN (or the TruMerit Qualifying Exam). For the NCLEX itself, your state board of nursing asks for its own credential evaluation, which is a separate process."
  },
  {
    question: "How does the VisaScreen relate to the EB-3 visa?",
    answer: "It is a federal requirement for the work visa. In the EB-3 process with Schedule A, your employer attaches your VisaScreen certificate to the I-140 petition together with your RN license and the job offer. That is why it is filed once you have passed the NCLEX and the visa stage is about to start."
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="VisaScreen for Nurses: Cost, Requirements and Steps (2026)"
  description="VisaScreen for internationally educated nurses: it costs $740, requires an approved English test and the NCLEX-RN, is issued by TruMerit (formerly CGFNS) in up to 3.5 business days and is used in the EB-3 visa."
  canonical="https://enfermerausa.com/visascreen-for-nurses/"
  type="service"
  faqs={faqs}
  alternates={[
    { href: "https://enfermerausa.com/visascreen-for-nurses/", lang: "en" },
    { href: "https://enfermerausa.com/que-es-el-visascreen-costo-requisitos-pasos/", lang: "es" },
    { href: "https://enfermerausa.com/visascreen-for-nurses/", lang: "x-default" },
  ]}
>
  <div class="py-12 md:py-20 bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
    <div class="max-w-screen-xl mx-auto px-4 md:px-8">
      <div class="max-w-3xl">
        <span class="text-emerald-700 font-bold text-xs uppercase tracking-wider bg-emerald-100 px-3 py-1 rounded-full">Stage 3: Visa and employment</span>
        <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-5xl font-extrabold text-gray-900 tracking-tight mt-4 mb-6">
          VisaScreen for Nurses: Cost, Requirements and Steps
        </h1>
        <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
          The VisaScreen is the federal certificate an internationally educated nurse needs before receiving a U.S. work visa.
        </p>
        <p class="text-base text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
          <strong>Short answer:</strong> it is issued by TruMerit (formerly CGFNS), the initial application costs <strong>$740 USD</strong>, it requires a passing score on an approved English test and — for registered nurses — a passing NCLEX-RN score. It is used in the EB-3 visa process together with your RN license and your job offer.
        </p>
        <p class="text-base text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-6">
          <strong>Cost:</strong> $740 USD for the initial application, according to the <a href="https://www.trumerit.org/support/fee-schedule" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-emerald-700 underline">TruMerit fee schedule</a>. Last reviewed: November 28, 2026.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-3">
          <a href="/sesion-informativa/" class="inline-flex items-center bg-emerald-600 hover:bg-emerald-700 text-white font-bold px-6 py-3 rounded-lg shadow transition-all gap-2">
            <span>Free information session (in Spanish)</span>
          </a>
          <a href="#visa-screen-steps" class="inline-flex items-center border-2 border-emerald-600 text-emerald-800 hover:bg-emerald-50 font-bold px-6 py-3 rounded-lg gap-2">
            <span>See the steps</span>
          </a>
          <a href="/que-es-el-visascreen-costo-requisitos-pasos/" class="inline-flex items-center border-2 border-emerald-600 text-emerald-800 hover:bg-emerald-50 font-bold px-6 py-3 rounded-lg gap-2">
            <span>Versión en español</span>
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="py-16 bg-white">
    <div class="max-w-screen-xl mx-auto px-4 md:px-8 max-w-4xl space-y-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900" id="what-is">What is the VisaScreen and what is it for?</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
        The Illegal Immigration Reform and Immigrant Responsibility Act of 1996 (section 343) requires certain foreign health-care professionals — including internationally educated registered nurses (RNs) — to complete a screening program before receiving a work visa, temporary or permanent. That program is the VisaScreen. When you complete it you receive the ICHP certificate (International Commission on Healthcare Professions), which satisfies the federal requirement.
      </p>
      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
        According to TruMerit, it is the only credentialing organization authorized by USCIS to issue this certificate for the nine health-care occupations that require it. Since November 1, 2025 the certificate is issued in digital format; a paper certificate is a separate service with an additional cost.
      </p>
      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
        ¿Eres enfermera hispanohablante? Esta misma guía está disponible en español: <a href="/que-es-el-visascreen-costo-requisitos-pasos/" class="text-emerald-700 underline">Qué es el VisaScreen: costo, requisitos y pasos</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900" id="visa-screen-cost">How much does the VisaScreen cost?</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto bg-white rounded-xl shadow-sm border border-slate-200">
        <table class="w-full text-left border-collapse text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-slate-100 border-b border-slate-200 text-slate-900 font-bold">
              <th class="p-4">Item</th>
              <th class="p-4">Cost</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-100 text-slate-700">
            <tr><td class="p-4 font-bold">Initial VisaScreen application (includes the digital certificate)</td><td class="p-4">$740 USD</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="p-4 font-bold">Paper certificate</td><td class="p-4">Separate service, additional cost</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="p-4 font-bold">English test (IELTS, TOEFL, PTE, OET, etc.)</td><td class="p-4">Charged by each testing company, separate from the VisaScreen</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="p-4 font-bold">Document translation by TruMerit (only if your school cannot translate them)</td><td class="p-4">Additional fee</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">
        Source: <a href="https://www.trumerit.org/support/fee-schedule" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="underline">TruMerit, Fee Schedule and Policies</a> and the <a href="https://www.trumerit.org/services/certification/visascreen-visa-credentials-assessment/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="underline">TruMerit VisaScreen page</a>. Last reviewed: November 28, 2026. Fees can change: confirm the current amount before paying.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900" id="visa-screen-requirements">Requirements: what the VisaScreen verifies</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">TruMerit reviews four things and asks for a few supporting documents:</p>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 text-gray-600 space-y-2">
        <li><strong>Comparable education.</strong> Your nursing education must be comparable to that of a U.S. graduate in the same profession. Transcripts must arrive directly from each institution where you studied nursing.</li>
        <li><strong>Valid, unrestricted licenses.</strong> TruMerit validates all licenses and registrations you hold or have held, in any country. The validating authority's signature must be less than three years old.</li>
        <li><strong>Approved English</strong> on an accepted test (table below), unless you qualify for an exemption.</li>
        <li><strong>For RNs only: a passing NCLEX-RN</strong> or the TruMerit Nurse Qualifying Exam.</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
        Supporting documents: high school diploma (waived if you graduated more than 10 years ago), documents in English (the translation must be done by your institution) and a passport-style photo, in color, square (51 mm x 51 mm), JPG under 5 MB, taken within the last 6 months.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900" id="english-tests">Accepted English tests and minimum scores for RNs</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
        HRSA updated the list on May 12, 2026. TruMerit does not accept at-home versions of any test, and TOEIC is no longer valid. If you took the test before May 12, 2026, the previous score requirements apply.
      </p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto bg-white rounded-xl shadow-sm border border-slate-200">
        <table class="w-full text-left border-collapse text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-slate-100 border-b border-slate-200 text-slate-900 font-bold">
              <th class="p-4">Test</th>
              <th class="p-4">Minimum score (RN)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-100 text-slate-700">
            <tr><td class="p-4 font-bold">IELTS Academic</td><td class="p-4">6.5 overall and 7.0 Speaking</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="p-4 font-bold">TOEFL iBT (new scale)</td><td class="p-4">4.5 overall, 4.5 Speaking and 3.5 in Reading, Listening and Writing</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="p-4 font-bold">Pearson PTE Academic</td><td class="p-4">55 overall, 76 Speaking and 62 in Reading, Writing and Listening</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="p-4 font-bold">OET</td><td class="p-4">360 Speaking, 340 Reading, 350 Writing and 320 Listening</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="p-4 font-bold">Cambridge (B2 First, C1 Advanced or C2 Proficiency)</td><td class="p-4">169 overall and 194 Speaking</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="p-4 font-bold">MET (Michigan English Test)</td><td class="p-4">58 overall and 59 Speaking</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">
        Source: <a href="https://www.hrsa.gov/office-global-health/foreign-healthcare-worker-requirements" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="underline">HRSA, requirements for foreign health-care workers</a>. The detailed comparison and exemptions are in our Spanish guide to <a href="/blog/posts/requisitos-ingles-visascreen-enfermeras-2026/" class="underline">VisaScreen English requirements</a>.
      </p>
      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
        <strong>Exemption:</strong> you do not need the test if your nursing education was completed in English (textbooks included) in the United States, the United Kingdom, Australia, Canada (except Quebec), New Zealand or Ireland. This exemption is uncommon among nurses trained in Latin America.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900" id="visa-screen-steps">VisaScreen steps and timeline</h2>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 text-gray-600 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Pass the NCLEX-RN</strong> (or the TruMerit Qualifying Exam). If you are an RN, the VisaScreen closes after this step. Guide: <a href="/licencia-de-enfermeria-y-examen-nclex-usa/" class="text-emerald-700 underline">RN license and NCLEX exam</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Take the approved English test</strong> with the scores in the table, with enough lead time: results have limited validity.</li>
        <li><strong>Create your TruMerit account and open the VisaScreen application</strong>, and pay the $740 USD.</li>
        <li><strong>Request the documents from the right parties:</strong> your school sends transcripts directly to TruMerit and each licensing authority signs its validation. Let them know the same day you open the application.</li>
        <li><strong>Upload your supporting documents</strong>: photo, high school diploma if applicable and your English results.</li>
        <li><strong>TruMerit reviews and issues the digital certificate.</strong> According to TruMerit, it is issued within up to 3.5 business days after all documents are received and validated from the source.</li>
        <li><strong>Use it in your visa process.</strong> Your employer attaches it to the I-140 petition of the EB-3 process (next section).</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
        <strong>Where the real time goes:</strong> the 3.5 business days count from the moment TruMerit has everything. What usually delays the process is what you do not control: schools slow to send transcripts and licensing authorities slow to sign. Open those requests in parallel.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900" id="visa-screen-eb3">VisaScreen and the EB-3 visa: how they connect</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
        The EB-3 visa with Schedule A designation is the usual path to permanent residence for nurses with hospital sponsorship. The VisaScreen is not the visa and does not make you eligible for it: it is the federal requirement that verifies your credentials. In the EB-3 process, the employer files the I-140 petition and attaches your VisaScreen, your license and the job offer.
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-600 space-y-2">
        <li><strong>VisaScreen:</strong> certifies that your credentials meet the federal standard. Issued by TruMerit.</li>
        <li><strong>EB-3 / I-140:</strong> the employer's petition for your green card. Processed by USCIS.</li>
        <li><strong>CES evaluation for the NCLEX:</strong> another process entirely — the one state boards of nursing request to declare you eligible for the exam.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
        More detail on sponsorship in <a href="/blog/posts/visa-eb3-enfermeras-green-card-hospitales-usa/" class="text-emerald-700 underline">Visa EB-3 for nurses</a> and on the <a href="/proceso-de-visa-y-relocalizacion-para-enfermeras/" class="text-emerald-700 underline">visa and relocation process page</a>.
      </p>
      <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">
        Sources: <a href="https://www.uscis.gov/working-in-the-united-states/temporary-workers/health-care-worker-certification" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="underline">USCIS, Healthcare Worker Certification</a> and <a href="https://www.trumerit.org/services/certification/visascreen-visa-credentials-assessment/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="underline">TruMerit, VisaScreen</a>. This page is informational and does not replace advice from an immigration attorney.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="py-12 bg-slate-50 border-t border-slate-200">
    <div class="max-w-screen-xl mx-auto px-4 md:px-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6">VisaScreen FAQs</h2>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
        {faqs.map((faq) => (
          <div class="bg-white p-6 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 text-lg mb-2">{faq.question}</h3>
            <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm leading-relaxed">{faq.answer}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="py-8 bg-emerald-900 text-white text-center">
    <div class="max-w-screen-xl mx-auto px-4">
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold mb-2">Want to review your full path: credentials evaluation, NCLEX, VisaScreen and visa?</h3>
      <p class="text-emerald-100 text-sm mb-4">In the free information session we review your case step by step — in Spanish.</p>
      <a href="/sesion-informativa/" class="inline-block bg-white text-emerald-900 font-bold px-6 py-2.5 rounded-lg shadow hover:bg-emerald-50 transition">
        Book the free information session
      </a>
      <p class="text-emerald-100 text-xs mt-4">Still working on the NCLEX? Practice at <a href="https://rnnclex.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="underline text-white">rnnclex.com</a>, the NCLEX preparation platform from Enfermera en Estados Unidos.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="max-w-screen-xl mx-auto px-4 my-8">
    <div class="bg-slate-100 p-4 rounded-lg border border-slate-300 flex items-center gap-4 text-xs text-slate-700">
      <span class="font-bold text-emerald-800 bg-emerald-100 px-2 py-1 rounded">✓ Verified Information</span>
      <span>Published by Enfermera en Estados Unidos (founder: <a href="/quienes-somos/" class="underline">Alejandra Martínez, RN, BSN, IBCLC</a>). Official sources: <a href="https://www.trumerit.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="underline">TruMerit</a> | <a href="https://www.uscis.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="underline">USCIS</a> | <a href="https://www.hrsa.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="underline">HRSA</a> | <a href="https://www.ncsbn.org/nursing-regulation/licensure/internationally-educated-nurses.page" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="underline">NCSBN</a>.</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
